                  {
                    "a": "(a) The lens formula is 1/f = 1/p + 1/q, where f is the focal length of the lens, p is the object distance and q is the image distance. (b) The lens formula does not differ from the mirror formula: the formula for the lens is exactly the same as the mirror formula. (c) All real distances are taken positively and all virtual distances negatively; the focal length of a convex lens is positive and of a concave lens is negative; the image distance is positive if the image is real (on the opposite side of the lens from the object) and negative if it is virtual.",
                    "q": "(a) What is the lens formula? (b) How does the lens formula differ from the mirror formula? (c) What are the sign conventions for lenses?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "(a) A magnifying glass is a convex lens: when an object is placed within its focal length, it forms a virtual, upright and magnified image on the same side of the lens, which the eye sees enlarged. (b) A magnifying glass has a short focal length so that its magnifying power is large (magnifying power M = 1 + 25 cm / f — a shorter f gives a larger M). (c) The power of a lens is the reciprocal of its focal length in metres: P = 1 / f (metre); its unit is the dioptre (D); a convex lens has positive power and a concave lens has negative power.",
                    "q": "(a) How does a magnifying glass work? (b) Why does a magnifying glass have a short focal length? (c) What is the power of a lens?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "(a) A simple microscope is a single convex lens (a magnifying glass); a compound microscope has two converging lenses — an objective lens of short focal length and an eyepiece of longer focal length — giving much greater magnification. (b) The magnifying power of a compound microscope is M = (L / f_o) × (1 + 25 cm / f_e), where L is the tube length, f_o is the focal length of the objective and f_e is the focal length of the eyepiece. (Note: the book prints this formula garbled as \"M = L / f_o = (1 + 25(cm) / f)\"; the correct form is the product shown here.) (c) In a simple microscope the single lens forms a virtual, upright, magnified image; in a compound microscope the objective forms a real, inverted, magnified image, which the eyepiece then magnifies again into a virtual image (finally inverted relative to the object).",
                    "q": "(a) What is the difference between a simple microscope and a compound microscope? (b) What is the magnifying power of a compound microscope? (c) How does the compound microscope differ from a simple microscope in terms of image formation?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "(a) A microscope magnifies nearby small objects; a telescope forms images of distant objects (stars, planets). The telescope's objective has a long focal length, while the microscope's objective has a short focal length. (b) In an astronomical telescope the objective (a convex lens of long focal length) forms a real, inverted image of the distant object at its focal point, and the eyepiece magnifies that image for the eye. (c) The magnifying power of an astronomical telescope is M = f_o / f_e, where f_o is the focal length of the objective lens and f_e is the focal length of the eyepiece.",
                    "q": "(a) What is the difference between a telescope and a microscope? (b) How does the telescope form an image of a distant object? (c) What is the magnification of a telescope?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "(a) The power of a lens is the reciprocal of its focal length (in metres). (b) P = 1 / f (metre); the unit of power is the dioptre (D). (c) A convex lens has positive power (+) and a concave lens has negative power (−).",
                    "q": "(a) What is the power of a lens? (b) How do you calculate the power of a lens? (c) What is the difference between the power of a convex and a concave lens?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "(a) Short-sightedness (myopia): a defect of vision in which a person cannot see distant objects clearly — the image of a distant object is formed in front of the retina. Long-sightedness (hypermetropia): a person cannot see near objects clearly — the image is formed behind the retina. (b) Short-sightedness is corrected by a concave lens (a diverging lens) of suitable focal length, which diverges the rays so that the image falls on the retina; long-sightedness is corrected by a convex lens (a converging lens) of suitable focal length.",
                    "q": "(a) What is the difference between short-sightedness and long-sightedness? (b) How are these defects corrected?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "(a) The least distance of distinct vision is the minimum distance from the eye at which an object can be seen clearly: 25 cm for a normal human eye. (b) The eye accommodates by changing the shape (curvature) of the eye lens with the help of the ciliary muscles: for near objects the lens becomes thicker (shorter focal length) and for distant objects it becomes thinner (longer focal length), so the image always falls on the retina.",
                    "q": "(a) What is the least distance of distinct vision? (b) How does the eye accommodate to focus on objects at different distances?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "(a) The near point is the closest point at which the eye can focus clearly (25 cm for a normal eye); the far point is the farthest point at which the eye can see clearly (infinity for a normal eye). (b) The ciliary muscles change the curvature of the eye lens: to focus on near objects the muscles contract and the lens becomes more convex (shorter focal length); to focus on distant objects the muscles relax and the lens becomes flatter (longer focal length).",
                    "q": "(a) What is the difference between the near point and the far point of the eye? (b) How does the eye lens change its shape to focus on objects at different distances?"
                  }
